    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Highly fractured, ellipsoidal inclusions of altered Aztec sandstone are identified in the Valley of Fire State Park, southern Nevada. Characterization of the elastic properties of both inclusion and unaltered sandstone has revealed an inclusion stiffness up to eleven times that of the unaltered sandstone. The elastic properties are used in a linear elastic fracture mechanics model that describes stress distributions in matrix with a circular inclusion subjected to a far-field stress. Model results show that within the inclusion there is a stress magnification of ca. 1.4 times that of the applied far-field stress. A preliminary 3-D elliptical model has shown a stress amplification of up to 3.2 times that of the applied far-field stress.This stress amplification is used in conjunction with the likely mode I fracture toughness of both materials to predict brittle failure of only the inclusion given some particular far-field stresses. These results help explain the unique fracture patterns observed in the inclusions relative to the unaltered sandstone and have significant implications to larger scale fracturing potential of various diagenetic zones within the Aztec sandstone.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
